## WootQL

Write WootQL yourself. It is a pipe language for logical Chatwoot resources, not SQL or Scheme.
Its stages are where, project, equality join, summarize, sort, and take. Inspect describe("wootql") for their exact syntax.
Inspect describe("resources") and the relevant resource for current fields, types, enums, and relationships. Never guess a field or join.
Use $parameters for values supplied to query-run. Parameters are data, never query syntax.
Every stage consumes the previous stage. project removes unselected fields; retain identity, downstream evidence, and ordering keys.
After summarize, only grouping keys and aggregate outputs remain. count() counts rows; other aggregates require a field.
Order sequence data explicitly and include a unique tie-breaker so pagination remains stable.
Do not add arbitrary date ranges, samples, or take limits. A successful query can still be the wrong dataset.
WootQL has no subqueries, window functions, per-group top N, or nested related collections. Retrieve an ordered flat set and finish in Scheme.
Query rows contain selected fields, not record refs. Build a ref from the resource type and database ID before fetch, related, or act.
